If a subscription lapses when its 30-day period has elapsed, use:

```js
function hasLapsed(lastPaidAt, now) {
  const THIRTY_DAYS = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
  return now.getTime() - lastPaidAt.getTime() >= THIRTY_DAYS;
}
```

Also, this measures exactly 720 elapsed hours, not 30 calendar days. If the subscription is calendar-based, add 30 calendar days to `lastPaidAt` and compare `now` with that expiry instead; daylight-saving transitions otherwise produce different results.

The comparison is correct **if both arrays contain only finite numbers** (with missing components treated as `0`).

Otherwise, it has a correctness defect: `NaN` breaks antisymmetry:

```js
compareVersions([NaN], [1]) // 1
compareVersions([1], [NaN]) // 1
```

Both directions cannot report “greater”. String components can also compare lexicographically (`"10"` is less than `"2"`).

Validate or normalize components before comparing:

```js
function compareVersions(a, b) {
  const len = Math.max(a.length, b.length);

  for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
    const x = a[i] ?? 0;
    const y = b[i] ?? 0;

    if (!Number.isFinite(x) || !Number.isFinite(y)) {
      throw new TypeError("Version components must be finite numbers");
    }

    if (x !== y) return x < y ? -1 : 1;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

