The comparison is correct **only if every present component is a finite number**.

As written, it mishandles mixed numeric types and `NaN`:

```js
compareVersions([1], ["1"]) // 1, but these represent equal versions
compareVersions([NaN], [NaN]) // 1, not 0
```

`x !== y` uses strict equality, while `x < y` may coerce types; with `NaN`, `<` is false, so the function returns `1` arbitrarily.

Validate or normalize components before comparing:

```js
function compareVersions(a, b) {
  const len = Math.max(a.length, b.length);

  for (let i = 0; i < len; i++) {
    const x = i < a.length ? Number(a[i]) : 0;
    const y = i < b.length ? Number(b[i]) : 0;

    if (!Number.isFinite(x) || !Number.isFinite(y)) {
      throw new TypeError("Version components must be finite numbers");
    }
    if (x < y) return -1;
    if (x > y) return 1;
  }
  return 0;
}
```

